          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When does guidance describe antivirals as worth considering for influenza?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes antivirals as most effective when started within 48 hours of symptom onset, and the benefit falls as that window closes. (Source: *Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment*, the national immunization and infectious diseases guidance.)\n\nGuidance is consistent about people who are severely ill or at higher risk. Observational evidence supports benefit beyond the early outpatient window for these patients. (Same source.) The passages I retrieved do not name which groups count as high risk, so I can't list them here.\n\nGuidance disagrees about otherwise healthy outpatients. Some bodies call antiviral use a reasonable option for reducing symptoms. Others say the effect on symptom duration is modest, the effect on complications and hospital admission is not well established, and routine use is not justified. (Same source.)\n\nA clinician can tell you whether treatment makes sense for your situation, especially if you are in a higher-risk group. If you have trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, or symptoms that get worse after they had started to improve, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
